  "geometry_notes": "Work in Angstrom-like world units, 1 unit = 1 Å; whole scene spans about ±5 units along X. Reaction axis is the world X axis, screen-right: Nu always on -X, X (leaving group) always on +X, central carbon fixed at the origin. Bond lengths as a function of progress t in [0,1]: C-X = 1.8 + 1.2*t*t*... use C-X = 1.8 at t=0, 2.4 at t=0.5, 4.0 at t=1; C-Nu = 4.0 at t=0, 2.4 at t=0.5, 1.5 at t=1 (symmetric mirror). C-H = 1.09 throughout. The three hydrogens sit on a cone about the X axis: H-C-X angle = 109.5 deg at t=0 (hydrogens tilted toward -X, umbrella opening toward Nu), 90 deg (exactly planar, perpendicular to the axis) at t=0.5, and 70.5 deg at t=1 (tilted toward +X) — this single angle sweep produces the whole inversion. Hydrogens are spaced 120 deg apart in the YZ plane; keep one hydrogen pointing straight down (-Y) so the wedge/hash reversal is visible from the default camera. Sphere radii: carbon 0.45, hydrogen 0.28, halide X and nucleophile Nu 0.62 (drawn as dark red-brown #A0301C in ball-and-stick). Electrostatic potential surface = a smooth blobby isosurface (metaballs / union of scaled spheres, radius ~1.9 around Nu and X, ~1.4 around C, ~1.1 around each H) so at t=0 it reads as one isolated sphere plus a two-lobed peanut, at t=0.5 as a wide symmetric three-lobed dumbbell about 7 units long, at t=1 as a two-lobed peanut plus an isolated sphere. Potential color ramp painted on that surface: most negative red #E8746B / #D9534F, orange #E8945A, yellow #E8E06A, green #8FD18F, blue #7F9FE0 for least negative. Charge weighting: at t=0 the Nu blob is fully red and the halide lobe red while the methyl lobe of the alkyl halide is blue/green; at t=0.5 both end lobes red and the central methyl band green-to-yellow; at t=1 mirror of t=0. Geometry caption text sits centered below the 3D scene: 'Tetrahedral' for t<0.25, 'Planar' near t=0.5, 'Tetrahedral' for t>0.75. The 2D reaction-scheme panel occupies the right third of the frame, three rows stacked